Because React checks props by reference, not by value. When it decides whether to re-render a memoized child, it compares each prop with `Object.is`. For objects that means "same object in memory?" Every time the parent renders, `{ color: 'red' }` creates a new object, so the child sees a changed prop even though the contents are identical.

```jsx
function Parent() {
  return <Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />
}
```

Two `{ color: 'red' }` literals are two different objects, so `Object.is` returns false.

Also, if `Child` isn't wrapped in `React.memo`, it re-renders whenever `Parent` re-renders, whatever the props are. So first check whether the child is memoized. If it's not, memo alone won't help until you wrap it.

Ways to fix it:

Hoist the object if it doesn't depend on props or state. A module-level constant is created once:

```jsx
const style = { color: 'red' }

function Parent() {
  return <Child style={style} />
}
```

Memoize it if it depends on render-time values:

```jsx
function Parent({ size }) {
  const style = useMemo(() => ({ color: 'red', size }), [size])
  return <Child style={style} />
}
```

Pass primitives if the child only needs a couple of values. `color` and `size` as separate props are compared by value, so they don't need `useMemo`.

Don't wrap everything in `useMemo` by default. It has its own cost and only pays off when something downstream is memoized or the object is used as an effect dependency. If you're on React 19 with the React Compiler enabled, it may do some of this automatically, so check your build setup before adding manual memoization.

Because the object is a new one every time the parent renders. `{ color: 'red' }` creates a fresh object on each render, and React compares props by reference (`Object.is`), not by contents. So `style` going from object A to object B counts as a change, even though both contain `red`.

The parent re-rendering is what triggers the child, though. A child without `React.memo` re-renders whenever its parent does, whether or not the object is new. The object identity only matters when the child is memoized, because memo is the thing that skips re-renders based on prop identity.

So the fix depends on your setup:

If the value is constant, move it outside the component so it's created once:

```jsx
const STYLE = { color: 'red' };

function Parent() {
  return <Child style={STYLE} />;
}
```

If the value depends on state or props, memoize it with those as dependencies:

```jsx
const style = useMemo(() => ({ color }), [color]);
return <Child style={style} />;
```

Often the cleanest option is to skip the object entirely and pass the primitive, like `color={color}`, then build the object inside the child. Primitives compare by value, so memo works without any extra wrapping.

If you're not sure whether the child is re-rendering because of the prop or because the parent re-rendered, React DevTools' "highlight updates when components render" setting will show you which components are running.

because react doesn't compare the values inside your object. it compares the object itself, by reference. every time the parent renders, `{ color: 'red' }` makes a brand new object, and a new object is never `===` to the old one, even with identical contents:

```js
({ color: 'red' }) === ({ color: 'red' })  // false
```

`React.memo` does a shallow check on props using `Object.is`. the object prop fails that check on every parent render, so the child re-renders:

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ style }) {
  return <div style={style} />;
});

function Parent({ count }) {
  return <Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />;
}
```

the same thing happens with `useEffect`/`useMemo`/`useCallback` deps and with context `value={{ ... }}`. those all compare by reference too.

fixes, pick the one that fits:

if the object doesn't depend on props or state, move it out of the component:

```jsx
const style = { color: 'red' };

function Parent({ count }) {
  return <Child style={style} />;
}
```

if it does depend on something inside the component, memoize it:

```jsx
function Parent({ color }) {
  const style = useMemo(() => ({ color }), [color]);
  return <Child style={style} />;
}
```

or pass primitives instead (`color="red"`), since strings and numbers compare by value and memo works fine with them.

one thing to check: if `Child` isn't wrapped in `memo`, it re-renders whenever `Parent` does no matter what the props are, so memoizing the object won't help on its own. you need both the memo and a stable prop. if you're on the React Compiler, it can do most of this automatic memoization for you.

Rekurzija je kad funkcija rješava problem tako što poziva samu sebe, s manjim ulazom, dok ne dođe do osnovnog slučaja koji se rješava bez daljnjeg poziva. Zamisli ruski set lutaka: otvoriš jednu, unutra je manja, pa još manja, dok ne dođeš do one koja se ne može otvoriti.

Primjer, faktorijel:

```python
def faktorijel(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return 1
    return n * faktorijel(n - 1)

print(faktorijel(5))  # 120
```

Svaki poziv smanjuje `n` za 1, a kad dođe do `n <= 1`, rekurzija staje i rezultati se množe unazad: 5 * 4 * 3 * 2 * 1 = 120.

rekurzija je kad funkcija poziva samu sebe, ali sa manjim dijelom problema. svaki poziv ide korak dalje prema osnovnom slučaju (base case), gdje se rekurzija zaustavlja. slično kao ruske matrjoške: otvaraš jednu za drugom dok ne dođeš do najmanje, pa onda slažeš nazad.

primjer s faktorijelom (5! = 5 * 4 * 3 * 2 * 1):

```python
def factorial(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return 1
    return n * factorial(n - 1)

print(factorial(5))
```

ispisat će `120`. osnovni slučaj je `n <= 1`, a svaki poziv čeka rezultat manjeg poziva i množi ga sa svojim `n`.

rekurzija je kad funkcija poziva samu sebe da riješi manji dio istog problema. svaki poziv ide korak dalje, dok ne dođe do baznog slučaja, a tu funkcija vrati rezultat bez novog poziva. bez baznog slučaja bi se beskonačno zvala i Python bi bacio `RecursionError`.

najlakše je zamisliti matrjoške: otvaraš jednu, unutra je manja, pa još manja, dok ne dođeš do one koja se ne može otvoriti.

```python
def factorial(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return 1
    return n * factorial(n - 1)

print(factorial(5))
```

za `factorial(5)` se događa ovo: 5 * factorial(4) → 5 * 4 * factorial(3) → ... → 5 * 4 * 3 * 2 * 1 = 120. ispis je `120`.
